before:
Promise.reject(reason) creates a rejected promise; handlers are attached immediately in this example. settledLabels maps the records to small display Strings. JSON.stringify serializes the arrays for exact output. No network work is started.
```javascript
async function settledLabels(promises) {
  const outcomes = await Promise.allSettled(promises);
  return outcomes.map(item => item.status === "fulfilled"
    ? "ok:" + item.value : "error:" + item.reason);
}
const inputs = [Promise.resolve("A"), Promise.reject("no")];
console.log(JSON.stringify(await settledLabels(inputs)));
try { await Promise.all(inputs); }
catch (reason) { console.log("all:" + reason); }
```
Output: ["ok:A","error:no"] then all:no.

after:
This example turns each outcome into a short label.
```javascript
async function settledLabels(promises) {
  const outcomes = await Promise.allSettled(promises);
  return outcomes.map(item => item.status === "fulfilled"
    ? "ok:" + item.value : "error:" + item.reason);
}
const inputs = [Promise.resolve("A"), Promise.reject("no")];
console.log(JSON.stringify(await settledLabels(inputs)));
try { await Promise.all(inputs); }
catch (reason) { console.log("all:" + reason); }
```
Output:
```text
["ok:A","error:no"]
all:no
```
`allSettled` keeps both outcomes. `all` fails because one input failed. These are practice Promises; no internet request is made.
